<h2>The recovery and the rival versions</h2>
<p>On 6 June 2025, officers of the Central Bureau of Narcotics, Chittorgarh-II, acting on specific information about opium moving on a black motorcycle, intercepted the applicant near village Pind, Tehsil Badi Sadri. A bag on the motorcycle yielded two packets — 1.799 kg and 2.694 kg, together 4.493 kg of opium. The applicant was arrested the same day and a prosecution complaint under Sections 8 and 18(b) of the NDPS Act followed in Crime No. 03/2025.</p>
<p>The defence case was built on splitting the recovery. The applicant claimed knowledge of only the smaller packet of 1.799 kg — below the 2.5 kg commercial threshold — and said the second packet had been handed over by one Raju Jat, described to him as pickle. The Investigating Officer, it was pointed out, had admitted in cross-examination that the recorded statements did not attribute knowledge of the second packet to the applicant. Procedural lapses were also urged: no Gazetted Officer or Magistrate at the search, and samples sent to the laboratory roughly twenty days after recovery.</p>

<h2>Why the second application failed</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>First application:</strong> dismissed as not pressed on 10 July 2026, with liberty to refile after the Investigating Officer was examined.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Second application:</strong> filed after that stage, invoking the IO's admissions and the custody period since 6 June 2025.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Result:</strong> dismissed on 28 October 2026 — the liberty did not relax the statutory test under Section 37.</div>
</div>
<p>The Court held that both packets were recovered together from the bag the applicant was carrying, and the explanation about Raju Jat — who could not be traced — remained the applicant's own version, uncorroborated by anything on record. At the bail stage, that explanation could not exclude the second packet from his conscious possession. Once the aggregate stayed above commercial quantity, the Court had to record satisfaction of reasonable grounds to believe the applicant not guilty, and it declined to do so.</p>

<h2>The procedural objections</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Objection</th><th>What the Court held</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 50 not complied with</td><td>Not attracted — recovery was from a motorcycle bag, not a search of the person, following Kallu Khan (Supreme Court) and Virendra Singh (Rajasthan High Court)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 52A sampling delayed</td><td>Samples were drawn before the ACJM, Kapasan, on 24 June 2025; whether the procedure and chain of custody were preserved is a trial question — delay alone does not justify bail, following Kashif and Bharat Aambale</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Prolonged custody</td><td>Relevant, but not sufficient on its own given the stage of trial and the statutory restriction</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What the order means in practice</h2>
<p>Three practice points emerge. First, liberty to refile after a witness is examined is procedural breathing room, not a finding that the fresh application will succeed; counsel should treat the second application as a fresh Section 37 exercise, built on concrete material that emerged in the interim. Second, a knowledge-based defence that seeks to split a composite recovery must be anchored in the prosecution record itself — an admission in cross-examination helps, but it cannot carry the day if the physical recovery remains unified and the alternative explanation rests on an untraceable third party. Third, objections under Sections 50, 52A and 55 remain live defences for trial, but the current line of Supreme Court authority confines their bail-stage utility to cases of fundamental, recovery-destroying lapses.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> In commercial quantity NDPS matters, the most productive bail-stage material is usually that which attacks conscious possession or the integrity of the recovery itself. Custody length and routine procedural delay, standing alone, rarely move a court bound by Section 37. These observations are tentative and for trial courts; nothing in a bail order decides guilt.</p>
</div>
